  82:main.c        **** // According to spwg, there's room for at least one "alternate timing"
  83:main.c        **** // This could be useful here, for saving a couple values to try-out...
  84:main.c        **** // e.g. 57Hz refresh worked perfectly, as far as I could tell, but I wanted
  85:main.c        **** // to see if I could bump that up... 60Hz looked great, until I was working
  86:main.c        **** // in Terminal (lots of contrast), and I noticed a few lines would skip...
  87:main.c        **** // Hopefully, if this works, having an alternate-timing of the
  88:main.c        **** // previously-working values will make switching back easy...
  89:main.c        **** // Haven't yet figured out how I'm going to implement this...
  90:main.c        **** // Alternate-timing kinda makes sense as the reliable fall-back
  91:main.c        **** // OTOH, if the main timing is set to something non-bootable, then what?
  92:main.c        **** // (If the experimental timing is put here in ALT_TIMING, then it could be
  93:main.c        **** // tested *after* booting with reliable values... OTOH, I've *also* found
  94:main.c        **** // that (maybe) the display itself has a better time syncing if it'd
  95:main.c        **** // already synced since power-up... so switching to a new (questionable)
  96:main.c        **** // value may work after the system has booted, but may not work upon a
  97:main.c        **** // fresh boot... 
  98:main.c        **** 
  99:main.c        **** // Anyways, these are the same macro-names as above, just with _ALT.
